            <div xml:id="d58">

                <pb facs="0076" n="62" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg62"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.001 Chiang Kai-shek/23a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Gauss</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-04-27T21:00:00-04:00">April 27, 1944—9
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>569. The Department has not communicated your 721, April 24, 4 p.m., to the War
                    Department but it is believed advisable that you suggest to General Hearn that,
                    if he has not already done so, he communicate with the War Department in regard
                    to the matter. If you deem it advisable you might again speak to General Hearn
                    along the lines of the final paragraph of your telegram suggesting that a more
                    official and direct method be devised for communicating messages from the
                    President to the Generalissimo than that which has been customarily employed;
                    that obviously the preferable method would be the personal delivery of such
                    messages to the Generalissimo himself; and that, in consideration of the fact
                    that the Generalissimo does not understand English, any subsequent message from
                    the President to Generalissimo Chiang be accompanied by a careful translation
                    into Chinese (you might offer the facilities of the Embassy for this purpose).
                    In this latter connection, Department believes that, all phases of the matter
                    considered, it is advisable that the Generalissimo have <pb facs="0077" n="63"
                        xml:id="pg63"/> an Embassy (or U. S. Army) translation into Chinese of
                    important messages from the President.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
